00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.790 align:start position:0%
 
the Federal Reserve is being pressed by  three proposals to change the way the  central bank operates now one has to do  with congressional oversight of the Fed  call it the audit the second would  require the Fed to adopt and follow a  specific equation in setting money  policy that's let's say that's an  equation alright and the third would  place new limitations on the Federal  Reserve's power to provide liquidity  during a financial crisis it's called  that the less liquid option  all right Federal Reserve governor jay  Powell spoke at Catholic University of  America the Columbus School of Law in  Washington today about these issues  j-pal is now with our chief Washington  correspondent Peter Cook for an  exclusive interview Peter Tim thanks  very much I am joined by governor jay  pal thank you for the time rare  opportunity for us to have a chance to  chat with you I want to ask you first of  all about your speech today you made a  pretty strong argument against audit the  Fed the push that we're seeing right now  in Congress the fact that you're making  this speech at all tells me that you and  your colleagues at the Fed see this as a  real threat right now so I really felt  that I had to speak out I feel very  strongly that the bill is misguided and  and frankly mislabeled it's really not  about a financial audit people hear  about and they think oh sure auditing  the Fed makes a lot of sense in fact is  we are financially audited I would know  that I chair the committee that oversees  the audits this is about a congressional  policy investigation of monetary policy  in real time in a way that is very  likely to interject short term political  considerations into the making of  monetary you think the independence of  the Fed is at stake with this debate  well I would say this is an incursion on  on the part of the Fed that needs to be  independent most of the Fed is just like  any other agency bank regulation doesn't  have any meaningful claim to  independence any different from another  federal agency monetary policy for great  historical reasons has traditionally  been insulated from short-term political  pressures and Congress did that in 1978  and the results have by limiting the GAO  s right to audit monetary policy the  result has been much lower inflation  which has been under control for 30  years to go back on that now given the  country's financial challenges going  forward it just makes no sense at all in  your speech though you did point out  that what the feds been up to the last  couple years  precedented actions the expansion of the  balance sheet to four point five  trillion dollar steps that you and and  others of the Fed have said made a  difference in rescuing the US economy  but at the same time those actions  because they were unprecedented don't  think in and of themselves obligate the  Fed in some way to some sort of review  by Congress or by others these were big  steps so these were unprecedented steps  and we absolutely welcome transparency  with open arms and and accountability as  well I went through in my speech all of  the ways in which we are transparent  about monetary policy we published  statements immediately after the meeting  we published minutes that are highly  detailed after that we you know we the  chair does press conferences should give  speeches I give speeches the other  governors do we testify we talking in  public you know nothing the Fed does it  happens on monetary policy without the  whole world watching so the idea that  there's some secret thing happening is  just it's just false it's in violent  conflict with the facts violent conflict  with the facts right now the facts are a  governor Powell you've got at least  three potential Republican presidential  candidates in the US Senate alone who  support audit the Fed at least the  concept of it are you worried that  politics are running against the Fed  right now so you know I I don't hold  myself out as an expert or a commentator  on politics my approach is really just  to state the facts and let people  understand and the most important thing  they need to understand is that this is  not about an audit everyone who hears  this thinks oh of course the Fed should  be audited this is this is not that at  all and people who are expert in the  area at people who follow this closely  really understand that pretty uniformly  this is about getting Congress into into  the meeting by meeting making of  monetary policy over time we have  learned that when that happens  inevitably elected politicians want more  accommodative policy results in higher  inflation and bad economic outcomes  since the GAO exclusion was enacted in  1978 we've had lower inflation it's been  under control why we would change that  now is a mystery to me are you in chair  Yellen you make a concerted effort  others at the Fed to try and meet with  members of Congress to satisfy their  concerns can you beat this basically so  I speak only for myself and this was my  idea to I put myself forward to do this  because I  I feel really strongly about this and  yes I engage with with elected members  all the time and I quite like doing it  and I did it in my prior lives as an  appointee under President George HW Bush  and I enjoy engaging with members and so  do the other governors of the Fed we're  up there quite a lot it's part of our  accountability and transparent  transparency to Congress we are  accountable we are transparent we  embrace that and so that's part of that  final question that's before I ask you a  little bit about the economy are you  confident if it were to pass in Congress  that President Obama would stand up and  veto this kind of legislation so that's  a great question but it's really not one  for me you know again I don't do the  politics I don't hold myself out as  understanding it I'm just going to  address the merits this is what I do I'm  just going to talk about the facts of  what the bill is the risks the history  and I think it's quite compelling and my  experience is if you put that out there  people of goodwill are going to get it  and they're gonna see that this is this  bill really threatens the performance of  the US economy and will be bad for the  American people let's talk a little bit  about the economy we had a jobs report  on Friday that was pretty good I was  there at the lock up at the Labor  Department how did that report if it did  alter your own view about where the US  economy is right now  so we've actually had a couple of pretty  good years back to back here growth in  2013 and 14 has been two and a half or  three percent job creation has been very  healthy and during that period in fact  last year was the most job creation in  15 years as you probably know and over  the last half of 2014 and into this year  the momentum is clearly clearly built to  the point where we're having job numbers  that were simply unthinkable when I  joined the Fed in 2012 and this last  this last report just kind of had  everything in it you would want it had  higher wages and had people coming back  into the labor force and very good job  creation so it's a great report it's  just one report though so what I'm  looking at is really continued progress  looking at a bunch of different factors  continued progress on getting back to 2%  inflation inflation is too low I want to  see I want to have some reason for  confidence that it's coming back up to  2% and you don't say you don't have that  yet so I think the time is coming but  it's not here I would want to see some  evidence I mean if you look at the  evidence right now is that core  inflation is down the  one's headline inflation is below one  that is a lot because of low oil prices  and the stronger dollar which drives  down import prices those should be  transient and when when the transient  effect wears off we should be back in  the middle one night 1/2 percent for  core inflation I would like to see  something that makes me confident that  we are in fact on a path back to 2%  Larry Summers said in an op-ed today a  message he delivered in Davos as well  the Fed should be sitting on its hands  at this point until it sees the whites  of inflation XY's and there were nowhere  near to that do you subscribe to that  are we looking at a potential liftoff  now further down into 2015 if not 2016  so I did read that piece of course and  again I would just say that what I want  is is data that give me some confidence  that inflation is moving back up towards  the 2% goal in the medium-term patience  in normalizing monetary policy that was  this key line from the statement before  what is your message about patience  right now how patient what can you be at  this point so I think patience is the  appropriate term I think that you know  low inflation through the channels that  I mentioned gives us some time although  it will be transient the effects of the  oil and air will probably be transient  assuming that the prices of oil and the  dollar stabilize and that gives us the  ability to be patient I think that's the  right thing to do it does feel very much  like there's plenty of slack left out  there in the economy  you've got people working part-time for  economic reasons you've got people who  want to come back into the labor force  or not currently in it and most of all  wages wages have been low and they wages  do not suggest any tightness in the  labor market yet so those things tend to  indicate that in fact the natural rate  is pretty low the natural rate of  unemployment might be lower than it's  been estimated to be is that word gonna  pose a communications challenge for you  all at some point you're gonna have to  drop that aren't you if you're gonna  make a move for example in June so you  know what we can do is try to be as  transparent and clear as possible about  the path that path is going to be  heavily data dependent at the end it's  going to be you know about the incoming  data and that's our role and you know I  think we've done pretty well with it in  in the last year or so and I think  that's what we can strive to do that's a  part of it that we control two outside  factors and  if I could first of all the stronger  dollar I know is the Treasury  Department's responsibilities worried  about the dollar but you all have to  factor it in in terms of the economy  overall isn't the stronger dollar having  in effect a tightening of financial  conditions and doing some of the heavy  lifting for you lissa just to echo that  the Fed doesn't is not responsible for  dollar policy as you mentioned that the  Treasury Department really is so we  don't talk about about dollar policy but  you know the dollar is strong in part  because of the strength of the US  economy compared to the rest of the  world so it's a sign of our strength and  the sign of the Fed is is you know  nearing that point at which it would be  appropriate to raise interest rates so  that's good it's a sign of strength  that's a positive thing it's also true  that you know it will weigh a little bit  on our exports and it will certainly in  the short term hold inflation down  through import prices so it might be a  slight headwind but I don't see it I  don't see it stopping the momentum we  really have an economy that's underway  here and sustainable pace you know  printing numbers again that that we  didn't even think about two and a half  years ago final question for them to see  if this is on your radar scanner to the  extent it is and that is what's  happening in Greece right now what  impact would it have on the US economy  if there was a Greek exit from the Euro  and and have you all started to factor  that in at all into your deliberations  so of course we do think about various  things and you know the Greek election  and the events since Greece of course  we're following that carefully and you  know there's lots of discussion of it  publicly markets are reacting and that  kind of thing these are really matters  for Europeans to sort out and as an  American policy maker I would just hope  that they can be sorted out in a way  that is not disruptive to financial  markets in the economy governor jay pal  really appreciate you joining us here on  Bloomberg thanks Peter

